{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "cbmz",
    "description": "The CBM-Z gas-phase mechanism exactly as WRF-Chem chem_opt = 170 (CBMZ_MOSAIC_KPP: CBM-Z through KPP, paired with 8-bin MOSAIC) integrates it: 139 reactions over 66 variable species plus the two fixed species H2O and M, translated from chem/KPP/mechanisms/cbmz_mosaic/cbmz_mosaic.eqn and cbmz_mosaic.spc of WRF v4.8.0. Species are in molec/cm^3 (the KPP units; WRF converts its ppmV chem array with conv = 1e-6 dens2con_a rho), so every rate coefficient is applied by mass action exactly as KPP applies RCONST.\n\nThe document carries the stoichiometry only. Each reaction's rate is a scoped reference to CBMZRateConstants, the component mounted here from ./cbmz_rate_constants.esm, which builds all 142 RCONST entries from the hand-authored rate-law templates of cbmz_ratelaws.esm. That component's own inline tests pin every coefficient to the dumped RCONST at rel 1e-9, so what this document adds -- and what its tests are about -- is the reaction network.\n\n139 reactions, not 142: the .eqn's identity rows are omitted. cbmz_mosaic.eqn {124:104}, {125:105} and {126:106} are HCl = HCl, NH3 = NH3 and H2SO4 = H2SO4 at rate 1 s^-1. Their net stoichiometry is zero, they exist only so that KPP keeps the three species in the mechanism for MOSAIC to find, and KPP's own generated Fun eliminates them -- A(124), A(125) and A(126) do not appear in cbmz_mosaic_Integrator.f90 at all. Writing them here would not be more faithful, it would be actively wrong: a 1 s^-1 production and an identical 1 s^-1 loss of H2SO4 (~1e8 molec/cm^3) added to a net tendency of order 1e3 would destroy that tendency by cancellation, an error the Fortran does not make. The three coefficients are still exposed by CBMZRateConstants, so all 142 dumped RCONST entries remain assertable. A reader looking for an identity reaction and not finding one has found this paragraph instead.\n\nTen rate coefficients read the state, and a coupling edge closes that loop. CBM-Z writes its peroxy-peroxy chemistry pseudo-first-order: reactions {127:107}..{136:116} are unimolecular decays of CH3O2, ETHP, RO2, C2O3, ANO2, NAP, ISOPP, ISOPN, ISOPO2 and XO2 whose rate coefficient is the sum over the whole peroxy pool, so each carries a hidden dependence on the other nine concentrations. CBMZRateConstants takes those ten as parameters -- that is what makes its coefficients assertable pointwise -- and the ten variable_map edges below drive them from this system's own species. The mechanism is not second-order in the peroxy radicals by accident of this spelling: it genuinely is, and the coupling is where that shows.\n\nFixed species. H2O and M are cbmz_mosaic.spc #DEFFIX species, declared here with constant: true (esm-spec 7.4): they take part in mass action -- O(1D) + H2O, the water-enhanced HO2 self-reaction, N2O5 + H2O, and M as the third body of O(1D) + M, O(3P) + M and OH + M -- but the mechanism does not advance them. They are therefore set through parameter_overrides, not initial_conditions. A handful of reactions also write M or H2O as a PRODUCT ({024:004} O3P + O3 = 0.42 M, {032:012} OH + M = HO2 + H2O, {039:019} OH + HO2 = H2O + 0.21 M); those are the .eqn's atom bookkeeping and, being products of a constant species, change nothing, exactly as in KPP.\n\nStoichiometric coefficients are the float32 roundings of the .eqn's decimals, for the same reason the rate coefficients are (FORTRAN_BUGS N106, the companion of N101). KPP's generated cbmz_mosaic_Fun writes its stoichiometry as default-real literals inside a real64 computation -- Vdot(9) = 0.008*A(68)+... -- so the mechanism evaluates 0.00800000037997961, not 0.008. Binding the decimals instead leaves a 4.8e-8 floor on every tendency; binding the roundings takes it to 5e-16. The exact decimals the chemistry intends are recoverable from cbmz_mosaic.eqn, and each reaction's reference.notes here quotes its .eqn line verbatim, so nothing is lost.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "gaschem",
      "wrf-chem",
      "cbmz",
      "kpp",
      "reaction-system"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R. A. and Peters, L. K. (1999), A new lumped structure photochemical mechanism for large-scale applications, J. Geophys. Res., 104, 30387-30415",
        "doi": "10.1029/1999JD900876"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Fast, J. D. et al. (2006), Evolution of ozone, particulates, and aerosol direct radiative forcing in the vicinity of Houston using a fully coupled meteorology-chemistry-aerosol model, J. Geophys. Res., 111, D21305",
        "doi": "10.1029/2005JD006721"
      }
    ]
  },
  "solver": {
    "stiffness": "high",
    "reltol": 1e-09,
    "abstol": 0.001
  },
  "models": {
    "CBMZRateConstants": {
      "ref": "./cbmz_rate_constants.esm",
      "model": "CBMZRateConstants"
    }
  },
  "reaction_systems": {
    "CBMZ": {
      "reference": {
        "notes": "WRF v4.8.0 chem/KPP/mechanisms/cbmz_mosaic/cbmz_mosaic.eqn (142 reactions, of which the three identity rows 124-126 are omitted here as KPP itself omits them from Fun) and cbmz_mosaic.spc (66 variable + 2 fixed species). WRF species names are the KPP names unchanged apart from case (cbmz_mosaic_wrfkpp.equiv)."
      },
      "species": {
        "H2SO4": {
          "units": "molec/cm^3",
          "default": 167793844.05364805,
          "description": "CBM-Z species H2SO4 (KPP VAR index 1); default = the surface-noon state of the reference column."
        },
        "HCl": {
          "units": "molec/cm^3",
          "constant": true,
          "default": 6853211902.568538,
          "description": "HCl (KPP VAR index 2), a RESERVOIR species of this mechanism. It is a KPP VAR species, but its only appearance in cbmz_mosaic.eqn is the identity row {124:104} HCl = HCl, which has zero net stoichiometry and which KPP eliminates from Fun along with the reaction; the generated code writes Vdot = 0 for it and this document says the same thing with constant: true. HCl is in the mechanism because MOSAIC partitions it into the aerosol, not because the gas phase does anything to it, so a coupled document that runs the aerosol owns this state -- which is also why the gas mechanism must not own it (CLAUDE.md: a component does not own state a consumer will also drive). Default = the surface-noon state of the reference column."
        },
        "NH3": {
          "units": "molec/cm^3",
