      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "scm_surface_noon_trajectory",
          "description": "The whole integrated state (64 species) of the reference column's surface layer at local noon (step 364 level 1, T = 309.11 K, M = 2.3344e19 molec/cm3, j(NO2) = 5.56e-3 /s), integrated 10 and 60 s from the state WRF handed KPP. 60 s is the model's own chemistry step chemdt, so this is exactly the call WRF-Chem makes, with the fastest photochemistry in the run.\n\nReference: the CONTINUOUS mechanism, not WRF's own step. Ten of CBM-Z's rate coefficients are state-dependent (the lumped peroxy-peroxy reactions {127:107}..{136:116} read the ten peroxy concentrations), and WRF calls Update_RCONST ONCE per chemistry step, so over its 60 s step those ten are FROZEN at the input state -- while this reaction system, whose coupling edges drive them from its own species, updates them continuously. The two are different trajectories of the same network. Verified rather than assumed: integrating this document's mass action with the peroxy coefficients held at their t = 0 values reproduces WRF's dumped var_out to 4.9e-9, and with them live it reproduces this document's own answer -- so the gap is the operator split, not a transcription error (FORTRAN_BUGS N107).\n\nSo kernels/cbmz_driver gained an ESM_NSUB loop that splits the step into nsub equal sub-steps and refreshes RCONST before each, converging the Fortran to the continuous limit that this document means; nsub = 1 reproduces the previous one-shot reference bit for bit. Convergence is clean and first order -- each factor of ten in nsub cuts the change by ten -- and the reference used here is nsub = 10000 at RTOL 1e-9, ATOL 1e-3 molec/cm3 (WRF itself runs KPP at RTOL 1e-3, ATOL 1.0, at which the trace species are not resolved at all). Richardson on the last refinement puts that reference within about 1e-7 of the limit.\n\nTolerance: rel 1e-5 with an absolute floor of 1e-3 molec/cm3, the reference integrator's own ATOL. rel 1e-5 is the solver-to-solver agreement expected between the KPP Rosenbrock and the binding's integrator; the rate coefficients underneath are already pinned to 1e-9 by the CBMZRateConstants tests. The absolute floor is not a slackened relative bound: 43 of the 64 integrated species exceed 100 molec/cm3 at this state and are pinned at 1e-5 relative, while the rest sit BELOW ONE MOLECULE PER CUBIC CENTIMETRE -- this idealized column's initial profile carries no aromatics, isoprene or terpenes, so API and LIM decay to 1e-92 and 1e-120 with nothing producing them, and a decaying exponential with no source has no relative accuracy in any integrator. Measured: over the 43 integrated species above 100 molec/cm3 the largest relative residual is 6.9e-08 (XPAR at t = 60 s), consistent with the reference's own remaining distance to the continuous limit; the trace species below one molecule per cm3 differ by many orders of magnitude relatively and are covered by the absolute floor. WRF's own one-shot step (nsub = 1) differs from this converged reference by 2.4e-04 at 10 s (XPAR) and 1.6e-03 at 60 s (XPAR), which is the size of the frozen-coefficient lag (FORTRAN_BUGS N107) at this state.\n\nHCl and NH3 are not asserted: they are reservoir species here (see their declarations), and the Rust runner materializes neither a state nor an observed for a constant species, so an assertion on one errors rather than reading the parameter (EarthSciAST #472, which also covers the constraint_equations route this document would otherwise have taken). What they would assert -- that the gas mechanism leaves them exactly where it found them -- is structural in this document rather than numerical.\n\nState: data/eqwefic/dumps/chem170_wrf/esm_dump_cbmz_kpp_364.json level k = 1; reference chem170_scm/replay/kptn_364_{10,60}_10000.json. The run is described in data/eqwefic/notes/chem170_scm_reference_run.md.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0,
            "end": 60.0
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1e-05,
            "abs": 0.001
          },
